---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { isAuthenticated } from '../../lib/adminAuth.mjs';

if (!isAuthenticated(Astro.cookies)) {
  return Astro.redirect('/admin/login');
}
---
<Layout title="SQL auditing">
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Security controls</p>
    <h1>SQL auditing configuration</h1>
    <p>Documented controls for tracking authentication, administrator changes, audit configuration, and data changes in the Futon Manufacturing database.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>What this scenario enables</h2>
      <p>Applied by the VM bootstrap</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card"><div class="label">Audit target</div><div class="value">Windows Application log</div><p>SQL Server Audit writes to <code>APPLICATION_LOG</code>, which AMA forwards through the <code>Event</code> table.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="label">Data changes</div><div class="value">SELECT · INSERT · UPDATE · DELETE</div><p><code>FutonManufacturingDbAuditSpec</code> tracks database-level activity by <code>public</code>.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="label">Server activity</div><div class="value">Logins and security changes</div><p>Login success/failure, password changes, principal changes, permission changes, role membership, and audit changes.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><div class="label">Forwarding</div><div class="value">AMA + DCR</div><p>The VM's Windows Application log is forwarded to the configured Sentinel workspace for queries and detections.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Reproduce on another SQL Server</h2>
      <p>Run as a controlled sysadmin operation</p>
    </div>
    <p>The scenario implementation is in <code>scripts/sql/Setup-FutonManufacturing.ps1</code>. Do not run the full bootstrap on an existing business server: it restores sample data and changes credentials. The following SQL is a first-time audit-only example; replace the database name and check existing audit definitions first.</p>
    <pre><code>USE master;
CREATE SERVER AUDIT FutonManufacturingAudit
  TO APPLICATION_LOG
  WITH (QUEUE_DELAY = 1000, ON_FAILURE = CONTINUE);
ALTER SERVER AUDIT FutonManufacturingAudit WITH (STATE = ON);

CREATE SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION FutonManufacturingServerAuditSpec
  FOR SERVER AUDIT FutonManufacturingAudit
  ADD (FAILED_LOGIN_GROUP),
  ADD (SUCCESSFUL_LOGIN_GROUP),
  ADD (LOGIN_CHANGE_PASSWORD_GROUP),
  ADD (SERVER_PRINCIPAL_CHANGE_GROUP),
  ADD (SERVER_PERMISSION_CHANGE_GROUP),
  ADD (SERVER_ROLE_MEMBER_CHANGE_GROUP),
  ADD (AUDIT_CHANGE_GROUP)
  WITH (STATE = ON);

USE FutonManufacturing;
CREATE DATABASE AUDIT SPECIFICATION FutonManufacturingDbAuditSpec
  FOR SERVER AUDIT FutonManufacturingAudit
  ADD (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON DATABASE::FutonManufacturing BY public)
  WITH (STATE = ON);</code></pre>
    <p class="warn">For production, narrow the database principals and objects instead of auditing every public database action. Review retention, event volume, privacy, and the audit failure policy for your workload.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Verify the controls</h2>
      <p>Run on the SQL VM</p>
    </div>
    <pre><code>-- Server audit state and destination
SELECT name, is_state_enabled, type_desc, on_failure_desc
FROM sys.server_audits
WHERE name = 'FutonManufacturingAudit';

-- Server-level action groups
SELECT sas.name, sad.audit_action_name
FROM sys.server_audit_specifications AS sas
JOIN sys.server_audit_specification_details AS sad
  ON sas.server_specification_id = sad.server_specification_id
WHERE sas.name = 'FutonManufacturingServerAuditSpec';

-- Database-level data actions
SELECT das.name, dad.audit_action_name
FROM FutonManufacturing.sys.database_audit_specifications AS das
JOIN FutonManufacturing.sys.database_audit_specification_details AS dad
  ON das.database_specification_id = dad.database_specification_id
WHERE das.name = 'FutonManufacturingDbAuditSpec';</code></pre>
  <h3>Log Analytics verification (KQL, not T-SQL)</h3>
  <pre><code>Event
| where TimeGenerated &gt; ago(30m)
| where EventLog == 'Application' and Source == 'MSSQLSERVER'
| where EventID == 33205
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, EventID, RenderedDescription
| order by TimeGenerated desc
| take 50</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Coverage and limits</h2>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li>Event ID 33205 contains SQL Server Audit action details, including data-change operations.</li>
      <li>Event IDs 18453, 18454, and 18456 supplement successful and failed login visibility.</li>
      <li>The admin page independently confirms operator actions through the forwarded Windows event stream.</li>
      <li>Audit success does not prove Key Vault synchronization; those are separate checks.</li>
      <li>SQL Audit records statements and principals, not a full row before/after history or proof of transaction commit. Use temporal tables or change data capture when row history is required.</li>
      <li>QUEUE_DELAY = 1000 queues delivery for up to roughly one second. ON_FAILURE = CONTINUE favors availability and can permit operations without an audit record during a target failure.</li>
      <li>Application-log collection is suitable for this training scenario; production workloads may need a dedicated audit file target, retention policy, and narrower object scope.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Layout>